<article class="env-lemma" id="08QJ">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/08QJ">Lemma <span data-tag="08QJ">92.5.6</span>.</a> Let $A \to B$ be a ring map. Let $\pi $, $\mathcal{O}$, $\underline{B}$ be as in (<a href="/tag/08PR" data-tag="08PR">92.4.0.1</a>). For any $\mathcal{O}$-module $\mathcal{F}$ we have </p>
  <div class="equation">
  \[  L\pi _!(\mathcal{F}) = L\pi _!(Li^*\mathcal{F}) = L\pi _!(\mathcal{F} \otimes _\mathcal {O}^\mathbf {L} \underline{B})  \]
</div>
  <p> in $D(\textit{Ab})$. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      It suffices to verify the assumptions of Cohomology on Sites, Lemma <a href="/tag/08RX" data-tag="08RX">21.39.12</a> hold for $\mathcal{O} \to \underline{B}$ on $\mathcal{C}_{B/A}$. We will use the results of Remark <a href="/tag/08QI" data-tag="08QI">92.5.5</a> without further mention. Choose a resolution $P_\bullet $ of $B$ over $A$ to get a suitable cosimplicial object $U_\bullet $ of $\mathcal{C}_{B/A}$. Since $P_\bullet \to B$ induces a quasi-isomorphism on associated complexes of abelian groups we see that $L\pi _!\mathcal{O} = B$. On the other hand $L\pi _!\underline{B}$ is computed by $\underline{B}(U_\bullet ) = B$. This verifies the second assumption of Cohomology on Sites, Lemma <a href="/tag/08RX" data-tag="08RX">21.39.12</a> and we are done with the proof. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>